course: euphemisms
lesson: 20
title: Soft words for hard things: money and poverty and work, firing and the economy
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: Banks and companies use dry, technical words so that debt and job losses sound like ordinary paperwork.
explain: A bank says "outstanding balance" for money you still owe, and "delinquent account" for a debt not paid on time.
explain: A company that sacks hundreds of workers may call it "downsizing", "rightsizing" or "restructuring", which is spin to make it sound sensible.
explain: When a boss is forced out, the press release often says he is leaving "to spend more time with family".
explain: For example, "We are streamlining the team" from a manager usually means some people are about to lose their jobs.
fact: "In arrears" means behind on payments you owe, such as rent.
fact: "Outstanding balance" means money you still owe.
fact: "Delinquent account" (US jargon) means a debt that has not been paid on time.
fact: "Subprime borrower" means a borrower with a bad credit history: a record of not paying back borrowed money on time.
fact: "Credit-challenged" (US) means having bad credit.
fact: "Negative cash flow" means spending more money than comes in.
fact: "Cash flow problem" often means having no money to pay the bills.
fact: "Let go" means fired.
fact: "Downsizing" means cutting jobs.
fact: "Rightsizing" is spin for cutting jobs.
fact: "Restructuring" means reorganising a company, usually with job cuts.
fact: "Streamlining" means cutting jobs or services.
fact: "Made redundant" (UK) means losing your job because the post was cut.
fact: "Laid off" (US) means dismissed from work because there is not enough work.
fact: "Furloughed" means sent home from work for a period, often unpaid.
fact: "Involuntary separation" (official US) means being fired.
fact: "Pursuing other opportunities" in a company announcement usually means fired or pushed to quit.
fact: "To spend more time with family" is often the official reason given when someone is forced to resign.
fact: "Between jobs" means unemployed.
fact: "In transition" means unemployed or changing jobs.
exercise: A letter says you are "in arrears" with your rent. What does it mean? | You are behind on payments | You paid in advance | You live at the back | Your rent went down
exercise: The bill shows an "outstanding balance". What is it? | Money you still owe | A brilliant score | Money the shop owes you | A perfectly balanced budget
exercise: A US bank calls an account "delinquent". What does it mean? | The debt has not been paid on time | The account holder is a criminal | The account is closed and empty | The account earns extra interest
exercise: Who is a "subprime borrower"? | Someone with a bad credit history | Someone who borrows submarines | A top customer of the bank | Someone who borrows small sums
exercise: A car dealer welcomes "credit-challenged buyers". Who are they? | People with bad credit | People who love challenges | People who pay in cash | People with lots of credit cards
exercise: A shop has "negative cash flow". What does it mean? | It spends more than comes in | Its cash machine is broken | It refuses to take cash from shoppers | Customers are unhappy
exercise: The café owner says he has "a cash flow problem". What is likely true? | He cannot pay the bills | His till is leaking coins | He has too much money | His water pipe burst
exercise: What does "let go" mean when a boss says it? | Fired | Allowed to go home early | Released from a hug | Given a holiday
exercise: The company announces it is "downsizing". What will happen? | Jobs will be cut | It will move to smaller desks | Its products will shrink | It will sell smaller clothes
exercise: A manager says the firm is "rightsizing". What does it mean? | Cutting jobs | Making the right size of shoes | Fixing mistakes in sizes | Hiring the right people
exercise: The bank is "restructuring". What usually comes with it? | Job cuts | A new building | Free money for customers | A new logo only
exercise: The council is "streamlining services". What does it mean? | Cutting jobs or services | Cleaning the river | Making services faster and bigger | Adding new streams
exercise: In the UK, someone "made redundant" has what? | Lost their job because the post was cut | Been made the boss of the whole team | Been told off for repeating | Been given extra work
exercise: In the US, a worker who was "laid off" has what? | Lost the job because there was not enough work | Taken a long nap at work after lunch | Been promoted | Been put on a bed
exercise: A worker is "furloughed" for three months. What does it mean? | Sent home from work for a while, often unpaid | Given a fur coat to wear at work | Sent on a paid holiday abroad | Moved to a farm
exercise: A form mentions "involuntary separation". What happened to the worker? | They were fired | They moved desks | They divorced | They lost a glove
exercise: "Mr Jones is leaving us to pursue other opportunities." What usually happened? | He was fired or pushed to quit | He won a new prize | He is going to college for fun | He is retiring rich
exercise: A minister resigns "to spend more time with family". What is often the truth? | He was forced to resign | He has a new baby | He wanted a long holiday | He moved house
exercise: "My uncle is between jobs right now." What does it mean? | He is unemployed | He has two jobs | He works in the middle office | He is walking to work
exercise: A neighbour says she is "in transition". What can it mean? | She is unemployed or changing jobs | She is on a train going to another city | She is translating a book | She is moving furniture
test: News line: "Carmaker to cut 2,000 jobs in major restructuring." What is "restructuring" hiding here? | Thousands of job cuts | A new factory roof | A change of logo | More cars made
test: "Dad got let go today, mum's being weird about it" — what happened to Dad? | He lost his job | He got a day off | He left a club | He was let out of hospital
test: A company email: "As part of streamlining, the Leeds office will close." What will happen to the Leeds workers? | They are likely to lose their jobs | They will get faster computers | They will swim in a stream | They will get a pay rise
test: A politician: "This isn't job cuts, it's rightsizing." Which is true? | It is still cutting jobs | It means hiring more people | It means making uniforms fit | It means paying people more
test: "boss says we're furloughed till March, so like paid holiday??" — is it a paid holiday? | No, they are sent home, often without pay | Yes, it is a paid holiday for everybody | Yes, they get double pay | No, they must work at home
test: A press release: "Our director leaves to spend more time with family." Gossip at the office says what probably happened? | He was forced out | He had triplets | He won the lottery | He moved next door to family
test: Gran reads a letter: "Your account is three months in arrears." What does it mean? | She is behind on payments | She is three months ahead | She has a bonus | The account is closed
test: Liv reckons "made redundant" means you did the same job twice. Right? | No, it means losing your job because the post was cut | Yes, it means repeating the same work twice over | Yes, it means getting promoted | No, it means getting a pay rise
test: "Mate, I'm not unemployed, I'm in transition, ok?" What is he really? | Out of work | On a bus | Learning a language | Moving house
test: The shop owner sighs: "Bit of a cash flow problem, lads, wages next week." What is going on? | He cannot pay the wages now | He is giving a bonus | The till is broken | The shop is closing for good
test: A bank calls a family "subprime borrowers". What does the bank think of them? | They have a bad credit history | They are its best customers | They borrow only small sums | They work for the bank
test: "the bill says outstanding balance £40 — outstanding means great, so all good?" — is all good? | No, £40 is still owed | Yes, it is a reward of £40 | Yes, they owe you £40 | No, the bill is wrong
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises an employee subject to "involuntary separation"? | The employee has been dismissed | The employee has transferred departments | The employee has resigned of his own accord | The employee has been granted leave
exam: It is given that an account is designated "delinquent" by a financial institution in the United States. Identify the principal meaning of this designation. | The debt has not been repaid when due | The account holder has been convicted of an offence | The account has accrued additional interest in the holder's favour | The account has been closed with a nil balance
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning a company announcement that a senior executive is departing "to pursue other opportunities". | The executive has in all likelihood been dismissed or compelled to resign | The executive has received an award | The executive has retired with substantial wealth | The executive has commenced a course of study for personal enjoyment and leisure
exam: Whereas an undertaking states that it is "downsizing", determine the consequence most likely to ensue for its personnel. | A reduction in the number of jobs | A reduction in the size of furniture | An increase in remuneration | The relocation of all staff to larger premises
